Subject: rtl_tcp: exit on SIGTERM even when a client is connected

rtl_tcp's signal handler sets do_exit and cancels the async read, but when
a client is connected that only ends the session: after the teardown the
main loop resets do_exit to 0 and listens again. MeshSat's reader stops
rtl_tcp with SIGTERM so that rtlsdr_close runs; on tesseract (18 Sep 2026)
the SIGTERM was swallowed this way, the process was SIGKILLed after 5 s
and the device was never closed. A real signal (non-zero signum; the
workers call sighandler(0) on socket errors) now ends the process after
the session teardown, through the existing out: path. [MESHSAT-1222]

--- a/src/rtl_tcp.c
+++ b/src/rtl_tcp.c
@@ -90,6 +90,12 @@
 static int llbuf_num = DEFAULT_MAX_NUM_BUFFERS;
 
 static volatile int do_exit = 0;
+/* Set only by a real signal (SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGQUIT), never by the
+ * workers' sighandler(0) on a socket error. A signal that lands while a
+ * client is connected used to end only that session: the main loop then
+ * reset do_exit and went back to listening, so SIGTERM was ignored and a
+ * supervisor had to SIGKILL, skipping rtlsdr_close. [MeshSat MESHSAT-1222] */
+static volatile int signal_exit = 0;
 
 
 void usage(void)
@@ -146,6 +152,8 @@
 static void sighandler(int signum)
 {
 	signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN);
+	if (signum)
+		signal_exit = 1;
 	fprintf(stderr, "Signal caught, exiting!\n");
 	rtlsdr_cancel_async(dev);
 	do_exit = 1;
@@ -654,6 +662,8 @@
 			free(prev);
 		}
 
+		if (signal_exit)
+			goto out;
 		do_exit = 0;
 		global_numq = 0;
 	}
